{{ template "chart.homepageLine" . }}

A Kasm Workspaces deployment has two halves. The **control plane** is what users log in to: the web
UI, the manager and API services, the session proxy, Guacamole, the RDP gateways, and the database.
The **agent** is what actually runs sessions: an operator, an `Agent` custom resource, and a session
proxy users' browsers connect to directly. Each half already has a chart of its own — `kasm-helm`
and `kasm-agent` — and each installs perfectly well alone.

This chart installs both as one release, with either half switchable off. It deliberately adds no
machinery of its own — no hook Jobs, nothing that reaches into a running deployment. The one
setting the two halves have to agree on (the **Kasm Authorization Domain**, so sessions on the
agent's hostname actually authenticate) is a plain value on a fresh install,
`kasm-helm.kasmConfig.authDomain`; on an existing database it stays a documented manual action.
Both paths are spelled out below and in the chart's install NOTES.

## What this chart installs

Neither dependency is aliased, so each keeps its own chart name as its values key and every value
either chart documents is set here nested under that key.

| Key | Chart | Default | What it is |
| --- | ----- | ------- | ---------- |
| `kasm-helm` | `kasm-helm` | enabled | The Kasm Workspaces control plane: web UI, manager/API, session proxy, Guacamole, RDP gateways, PostgreSQL. |
| `kasm-agent` | `kasm-agent` | enabled | The Kubernetes agent umbrella: agent operator, OpenTelemetry collector, `Agent` resource and its session proxy, plus the optional per-feature cluster infrastructure. |

On top of those, this chart owns exactly one thing of its own: an `extraObjects` escape hatch.

### The toggle matrix

| `kasm-helm.enabled` | `kasm-agent.enabled` | What you get |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `true` | `true` | The full stack in one release. The default, and what the worked example below builds. |
| `true` | `false` | Control plane only — identical to installing `kasm-helm` on its own. Use it when agents live in other clusters, or are VM agents, or are added later. Still set `kasm-helm.kasmConfig.authDomain` at install time, ahead of the first agent. |
| `false` | `true` | Agent only — identical to installing `kasm-agent` on its own. Use it for a second agent cluster registering with a manager that already exists (another cluster, a VM deployment, a Kasm-hosted one). The auth-domain step does not apply: there is no control plane here. |
| `false` | `false` | Nothing but `extraObjects`. |

Because neither dependency is aliased, the dependencies *inside* `kasm-agent` keep their own
aliases and conditions, and those stay reachable from the top: `kasm-agent.operator.enabled`,
`kasm-agent.nodePrep.enabled`, `kasm-agent.gpuOperator.enabled`, and so on all work from a
top-level values file.

## The Kasm Authorization Domain, and why this chart exists

Kasm scopes its session cookie with a global setting, `kasm_auth_domain`, which defaults to
`$request_host$` — the hostname of whatever served the page. With a Kubernetes agent, users' browsers
connect to the **agent's** session proxy directly, on a different hostname from the control plane.
The cookie is scoped to the control plane's hostname, the browser never sends it to the agent, and
every session connection fails with a 401.

The fix is to set `kasm_auth_domain` to a parent domain covering both hostnames. With
`kasm.example.com` and `sessions.example.com`, that is `example.com`. Pick the two hostnames as
siblings under one parent from the very start; there is no fixing this later without renaming one.

### On a fresh install: a value

`kasm-helm.kasmConfig.authDomain` sets it at database initialization, so the very first install
comes up already able to authenticate sessions on the agent's hostname:

```yaml
kasm-helm:
  kasmConfig:
    authDomain: example.com
```

It needs no `generatePreseed`, and it is applied last against the finished seed file, so it wins
over `existingDefaultPropertiesSecret` and over anything `kasmConfig.config` sets. The quickstart
below includes it.

### On an existing database: three API calls

The db-init Job only seeds a database it initializes, so on a control plane that is already running
the value has to be applied through the admin UI or the admin API instead. This chart deliberately
ships no automation for it — no hook Jobs are baked in. Once the control plane answers on
`https://<kasm-helm.publicAddr>/api/__healthcheck`, set the value in the admin UI (Settings → Auth),
or with three API calls:

1. `POST /api/authenticate` — JSON `{"username": "admin@kasm.local", "password": "<admin
   password>"}` → response field `token`.
2. `POST /api/admin/get_settings` — JSON `{"username": ..., "token": ...}` → find the
   `settings[]` entry with `name == "kasm_auth_domain"` and note its `setting_id`.
3. `POST /api/admin/update_setting` — JSON `{"username": ..., "token": ..., "setting_id": ...,
   "value": "<parent domain>"}` (top-level keys — a `target_setting` wrapper does not work).

The chart's post-install NOTES repeat these steps with the release's actual hostnames filled in
whenever `authDomain` is left unset.

## Quickstart: the whole stack in one namespace

This is the complete configuration for a single-namespace, single-cluster install. It is also
checked in as `examples/kasm-agent/platform-values.yaml`, ready to copy.

```yaml
kasm-helm:
  enabled: true
  deploymentSize: small
  publicAddr: kasm.example.com

  # ClusterIP plus the chart's own Ingress. Never LoadBalancer next to an ingress controller that
  # already binds the node's :443 — the chart rejects that combination outright.
  proxyService:
    type: ClusterIP
  ingress:
    enabled: true
    ingressClassName: traefik
    backendProtocol: http
  certificate:
    secretName: kasm-tls

  kasmSecrets:
    passwords:
      # Pinning the token lets the agent half of this same release reference it without anyone
      # reading it back after install. Leave the other keys unset so they stay random.
      manager-token: "change-me-agent-registration-token"

  # Two zone defaults break Kubernetes-agent sessions. Both are preseed-able on a FRESH database.
  kasmZones:
    - name: default
      proxy_hostname: kasm.example.com
      # Browsers connect straight to the agent's hostname instead of being relayed through the
      # control-plane proxy, which forwards the original Host header and 404s behind host-routing
      # ingress.
      proxy_connections: false
      # Where the agent-side session proxy validates sessions; the $request_host$ default would
      # point it at the agent's own hostname.
      upstream_auth_address: kasm.example.com
  kasmConfig:
    generatePreseed: true
    # The parent domain of publicAddr and the agent's publicHostname. Seeded at database
    # initialization, so a fresh install needs no post-install auth-domain step.
    authDomain: example.com

kasm-agent:
  enabled: true
  agent:
    manager:
      hostname: kasm.example.com
      # One namespace, one release: the agent reads the control plane's own Secret directly. No
      # copying, nothing to keep in sync.
      existingTokenSecret: kasm-secrets
      tokenSecretKey: manager-token
    publicHostname: sessions.example.com
    zone: default
    sessionProxy:
      certSecretName: kasm-session-proxy-tls
    ingress:
      enabled: true
      className: traefik
      tls:
        - secretName: kasm-agent-public-tls
          hosts:
            - sessions.example.com

  # The agent umbrella's baseline policies model only the agent stack's traffic; in a shared
  # namespace they would cut the control plane off.
  networkPolicies:
    enabled: false
```

Note `existingTokenSecret: kasm-secrets` — that is `<release>-secrets` for a release named `kasm`.
Adjust it if you name the release something else.

### Install

The chart is published as an OCI artifact whose package embeds all of its dependencies — both
halves and every subchart under them — so no `helm dependency build` is needed. This is the
simplest path:

```console
helm install kasm oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-platform \
  --version {{ .Version }} -n kasm --create-namespace \
  -f examples/kasm-agent/platform-values.yaml --timeout 20m
```

Or, from a checkout of this repository, stage the dependencies first — inside-out, because
`kasm-agent`'s own vendored dependencies must exist before `kasm-platform` archives it (on a fresh
clone neither is populated; `make deps-agent` does both):

```console
helm dependency build charts/kasm-agent
helm dependency build charts/kasm-platform
helm install kasm charts/kasm-platform -n kasm --create-namespace \
  -f examples/kasm-agent/platform-values.yaml --timeout 20m
```

No namespace label is required. The operator's per-workspace NetworkPolicy admits the session proxy
**by podSelector**, and all session and manager traffic flows through it, so sessions work with no
namespace labels even here, where the manager shares the namespace. Label it `kasm.com/role=manager`
only if something in the namespace genuinely needs *direct* ingress to workspace pods — which
normally does not occur. Neither chart sets the label.

```console
# Optional, and only for that narrow direct-ingress case:
kubectl label namespace kasm kasm.com/role=manager
```

The operator's five CustomResourceDefinitions arrive with that install, from the operator chart's
`crds/` directory — created once, never upgraded by a later `helm upgrade kasm`. To put them under
Helm's control instead, install the [`kasm-agent-crds`](../kasm-agent-crds) chart as its own release
**before** the command above, and upgrade it before this release thereafter; without it, apply schema
changes out of band with `kubectl apply --server-side -f charts/kasm-agent-operator/crds/`. See
[`charts/kasm-agent-operator/README.md`](../kasm-agent-operator/README.md#customresourcedefinition-lifecycle)
for both paths.

If `kasm-agent.nodePrep`, `kasm-agent.videoDevicePlugin`, or `kasm-agent.egressInstaller` is
enabled, the namespace also has to permit the `privileged` Pod Security Standard — which, in a
shared namespace, then covers the control-plane pods too (`nodePrep` stops rendering a privileged
DaemonSet here entirely once its only module is delegated to the Kernel Module Management operator
and node tuning is off — see [KMM mode](../kasm-node-prep/README.md#kmm-mode) — but
`videoDevicePlugin` still needs the label):

```console
kubectl label namespace kasm pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=privileged --overwrite
```

`egressInstaller` (per-session VPN egress, off by default) asks for more than that label: its
DaemonSet runs with `hostPID: true` and `hostNetwork: true` as well as a privileged container, so
any admission policy that blanket-disallows host namespaces rejects it even in a `privileged`
namespace. Read [`charts/kasm-egress-installer/README.md`](../kasm-egress-installer/README.md)
before enabling it — in a shared namespace that decision applies to the control plane too.

### Verify

```console
# Both halves rolled out
kubectl get pods -n kasm
kubectl get agents.agent.kasm.com -n kasm

# The Kasm Authorization Domain came from kasm-helm.kasmConfig.authDomain at init. On a
# pre-existing database, set it by hand first (see above) before connecting to sessions.

# The admin password
kubectl get secret kasm-secrets -n kasm -o jsonpath='{.data.admin-password}' | base64 -d; echo
```

Then sign in at `https://kasm.example.com` as `admin@kasm.local`, confirm the agent shows up under
Infrastructure, and launch a session — it should land on `https://sessions.example.com` without a
401.

For a narrated, end-to-end walkthrough of this quickstart — two commands to a working session,
with measured timings — see the [demo runbook](../../examples/kasm-agent/demo-runbook.md). For the
alternative **two-namespace** layout (control plane and agent as separate releases, with the
umbrella's NetworkPolicies enforced), deploy the example pair
[`single-cluster-control-plane-values.yaml`](../../examples/kasm-agent/single-cluster-control-plane-values.yaml)
and [`single-cluster-agent-values.yaml`](../../examples/kasm-agent/single-cluster-agent-values.yaml)
against the `kasm-helm` and `kasm-agent` charts respectively.

## Going deeper

This chart is a composition, so almost every question about it is really a question about one of
its two dependencies:

* **[`charts/kasm-helm/README.md`](../kasm-helm/README.md)** — every value under `kasm-helm:`:
  sizing, ingress and routes, certificates, the database (in-cluster or external), backups, the
  preseed, trusted CA bundles, OpenShift.
* **[`charts/kasm-agent/README.md`](../kasm-agent/README.md)** — every value under `kasm-agent:`,
  and in particular its **"Running alongside the kasm-helm control plane"** section, which is the
  long-form version of the pairing this chart automates: the zone, the registration token, the
  manager address, the hostname split, the optional namespace label, and the two zone settings. Its
  "Cluster preparation checklist", "Workspace node best practices", and "Airgapped installation"
  sections apply here unchanged. When an airgapped webcam module is delegated to the Kernel Module
  Management operator, that chain ends at the node-prep chart's
  **[Airgapped KMM (prebuilt modules)](../kasm-node-prep/README.md#airgapped-kmm-prebuilt-modules)**
  runbook (`make kmm-install-mirrored` for the operator's own images).

## Versioning

The two dependencies are pinned to exact versions in `Chart.yaml`. `kasm-helm` is versioned on its
own release cadence, so **when the control plane chart's version is bumped, the `kasm-helm`
dependency version here has to be bumped in lockstep**, and `helm dependency update
charts/kasm-platform` run to refresh `Chart.lock`. Without both, `helm dependency build` fails to
resolve the local dependency.

## Uninstall, in two steps

`helm uninstall` deletes the `Agent` custom resources and the operator that clears their finalizers
at the same time. If the operator goes first, the deletions hang — or worse, a later operator
install processes the stale deletion and tears down a live agent. Always delete the custom
resources first and let the operator clean them up:

```console
# End any live sessions first — KasmWorkspace resources carry an operator-cleared finalizer too.
kubectl delete kasmworkspaces.agent.kasm.com --all -n kasm --wait
kubectl delete agents.agent.kasm.com --all -n kasm --wait
helm uninstall kasm -n kasm
```

The control plane's PersistentVolumeClaims (the database, and any backup volume) are not removed by
`helm uninstall`. Delete them deliberately, once you are sure.

## Publishing

This chart is published to `oci://registry-1.docker.io/kasmweb/kasm-platform`, alongside
`kasm-agent`, `kasm-agent-crds` and `kasm-egress-installer` — built by the `helm-build-agent`
GitLab job on `develop` and `release/*`, then pushed by the manual `helm-deploy-agent-docker-hub`
job. See **Publishing** in the [`kasm-agent` README](../kasm-agent/README.md) for the full set.

**`make deps-agent` has to run before packaging, and the order inside it matters here more than
anywhere else.** `helm package` does not resolve dependencies — it archives whatever `charts/`
already holds. `deps-agent` stages `charts/kasm-agent` *first* and only then this chart, because
packaging this one archives `kasm-agent` off disk; reversed, the `kasm-agent` embedded here is
missing all nine of its own dependencies and the composed release installs a hollow agent half:

```console
make deps-agent package-agent-all
# -> dist/kasm-platform-{{ .Version }}.tgz + the other three
```

**The lockstep rule in [Versioning](#versioning) above is enforced, not just documented.**
`make version-check-agent` (`scripts/agent_versions.py --check`) fails when either `file://` pin
here has fallen behind the version the referenced chart declares — the `kasm-helm` pin is the one
that goes stale on a schedule, since every `make readme CHART_VERSION=...` moves the control plane
chart without touching this file. It runs inside `make test` and again at the top of the CI package
job, because a stale pin is only half-loud: `helm dependency build` refuses it, but `helm package`
against an already-staged `charts/` directory exits 0 and embeds the *old* archive. The fix moves
the chart and every pin naming it together:

```console
python3 scripts/agent_versions.py --bump kasm-agent 0.2.0 --write   # dry run without --write
helm dependency update charts/kasm-agent charts/kasm-platform       # refresh Chart.lock
```

`kasm-helm`'s own version stays owned by `scripts/set_versions.py`; after a control plane bump,
re-run the `--bump` above for `kasm-helm` to catch this chart's pin up.

At `{{ .Version }}` / appVersion `{{ .AppVersion }}` this is a preview rather than a moving release line, so
every build produces the same version. The publish job refuses to overwrite a version already in
the registry unless the pipeline sets `FORCE_REPUBLISH=true`.

